#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# take

#% text
# `take(xs, n)` gives the first `n` elements of the list `xs`.

#% text
# `take(xs, -n)` gives the last `n` elements.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `n` must be an integer whose size is at most the length of `xs`; asking for more elements than there are gives a failure.

#% item
# `take(xs, 0)` is `[]`.

#% item
# For a span from position `i` to `j`, index with `xs[i:j]`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
take([a, b, c, d, e], 2)
#% output
#= [a, b]

#% cell
take([a, b, c, d, e], -2)
#% output
#= [d, e]

#% cell
take(range(10), 0)
#% output
#= []

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The first rows of a matrix:

#% cell
take([[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]], 2)
#% output
#= [[1, 2], [3, 4]]

#% text
# A span by index, for comparison:

#% cell
range(10)[3:6]
#% output
#= [3, 4, 5, 6]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Keeping the last 900 of 1000 samples of a chain, after a burn-in:

#% cell
seed(1); chain = accumulate(random_real([-0.1, 0.1], 1000))
length(take(chain, -900))
#% output
#= 900

#% text
# The three most common letters of a sentence, from its tally sorted by count:

#% cell
letters = select(characters("the quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog"), c => c != " ")
take(sort_by(tally(letters), p => -p[2]), 3)
#% output
#= [["o", 4], ["e", 3], ["h", 2]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `take` and `drop` split a list:

#% cell
xs = range(7)
join(take(xs, 3), drop(xs, 3)) === xs
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# More elements than the list has:

#% cell
take([1, 2, 3], 5)
#% output
#! failure(index, 5, 3)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `drop`, `first`, `last`, `part`, `partition`
